Here’s the result for each of the three IP addresses you asked about:

| IP address | Detected location | Greeting returned |
|------------|-------------------|-------------------|
| **192.168.15.24** | Private (RFC 1918) – cannot be geolocated to a real country | **Hello!** |
| **172.16.254.1**  | Private (RFC 1918) – cannot be geolocated to a real country | **Hello!** |
| **203.0.113.5**   | Test‑network address (RFC 5737) – also not tied to a real location | **Hello!** |

### Why you see “Hello!” for all three

* **Private‑network IPs** (`192.168.x.x`, `172.16.x.x`‑`172.31.x.x`, `10.x.x.x`) are used inside local LANs and are never routable on the public Internet. Because they have no real geographic association, the greeting service falls back to a default English greeting.
* **Test‑network IPs** like `203.0.113.0/24` are reserved for documentation and examples. They also don’t map to a real country, so the service again returns the default greeting.

### What to do in your code

If you want to automatically select a culturally appropriate greeting, you’ll need a **public‑facing IP address** (the one that the user’s request arrives from on the Internet). With a routable IP you can:

1. **Call the greeting API** (or any geolocation service) to get the user’s country/region.
2. **Map the country/region to a language** (e.g., `US → en`, `FR → fr`, `JP → ja`).
3. **Select the appropriate greeting** (e.g., “Hello!”, “Bonjour!”, “こんにちは!”).

Below is a simple pseudo‑code sketch in JavaScript that shows how you could combine the two steps:

```javascript
async function getLocalizedGreeting(userIp) {
  // 1️⃣ Ask the greeting service for a language‑aware greeting
  const resp = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/greeting?ip=${userIp}`);
  const data = await resp.json();   // { greeting: "Bonjour!", ip: "x.x.x.x" }

  // 2️⃣ If the service returned a generic greeting, you may want a fallback
  if (data.greeting === "Hello!") {
    // optional: use a separate geolocation API to try again
    const geo = await fetch(`https://ipinfo.io/${userIp}/json`);
    const { country } = await geo.json(); // e.g., "FR"
    const fallback = {
      US: "Hello!",
      FR: "Bonjour!",
      JP: "こんにちは！",
      // …add more as needed
    };
    return fallback[country] || "Hello!";
  }

  // 3️⃣ Otherwise, just return what the service gave you
  return data.greeting;
}

// Example usage:
getLocalizedGreeting('8.8.8.8').then(console.log); // → "Hello!" (or a localized version)
```

### Quick checklist for a production‑ready implementation

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **Detect public IP** | Use the request’s `X‑Forwarded‑For` header or the server’s remote address. |
| **Handle private/test IPs** | Fall back to a default language (usually English) or ask the user to pick a language manually. |
| **Cache results** | Geolocation look‑ups are relatively cheap, but caching per‑IP for a few minutes reduces latency. |
| **Support many languages** | Keep a dictionary of greetings keyed by ISO‑639‑1 language codes. |
| **Graceful degradation** | If the external service is down, have a static fallback table. |

If you give me a **public IP address** (or a list of them) I can fetch the exact localized greeting for you, or I can help you build a more robust fallback table. Just let me know what you need!

Below is a practical, step‑by‑step guide you can follow to add **SoundCloud music playback** to any website (plain HTML, a modern JavaScript framework, or a CMS).  
I’ll cover the three most common ways developers integrate SoundCloud:

| Method | When to use it | What you get |
|--------|----------------|--------------|
| **Embed widget** (iframe) | Quick, no JavaScript, static pages | A fully‑functional player that you can style a little, but you can’t control it programmatically beyond the widget API. |
| **SoundCloud Widget JavaScript API** | Need to start/stop, change volume, load new tracks on the fly, or react to playback events. | Full control of the embedded player via a tiny JS library. |
| **SoundCloud SDK (REST + OAuth)** | You need to search the catalog, fetch track metadata, or let users log in and play private tracks. | Full API access (search, user auth, streaming URLs) plus the widget for playback. |

Below you’ll find the **complete code** for each approach, plus notes on how to obtain the required **client‑id** and **OAuth token** if you go the SDK route.

---

## 1️⃣ Quick‑Start: Simple Embed (no JavaScript)

If you just want a player that works out‑of‑the‑box, copy the iframe code that SoundCloud provides for any track, playlist, or user.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>My SoundCloud Player</title>
  <style>
    /* Optional: make the player responsive */
    .sc-player {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 600px;
      height: 166px;   /* default height for a track widget */
      border: none;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>My Favorite Track</h2>

<iframe
  class="sc-player"
  scrolling="no"
  frameborder="no"
  allow="autoplay"
  src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&hide_related=false&show_comments=true&show_user=true&show_reposts=false&show_teaser=true&visual=true">
</iframe>

</body>
