Below is a practical, step‑by‑step guide you can follow to add **SoundCloud music playback** to any website (plain HTML, a modern JavaScript framework, or a CMS).  
I’ll cover the three most common ways developers integrate SoundCloud:

| Method | When to use it | What you get |
|--------|----------------|--------------|
| **Embed widget** (iframe) | Quick, no JavaScript, static pages | A fully‑functional player that you can style a little, but you can’t control it programmatically beyond the widget API. |
| **SoundCloud Widget JavaScript API** | Need to start/stop, change volume, load new tracks on the fly, or react to playback events. | Full control of the embedded player via a tiny JS library. |
| **SoundCloud SDK (REST + OAuth)** | You need to search the catalog, fetch track metadata, or let users log in and play private tracks. | Full API access (search, user auth, streaming URLs) plus the widget for playback. |

Below you’ll find the **complete code** for each approach, plus notes on how to obtain the required **client‑id** and **OAuth token** if you go the SDK route.

---

## 1️⃣ Quick‑Start: Simple Embed (no JavaScript)

If you just want a player that works out‑of‑the‑box, copy the iframe code that SoundCloud provides for any track, playlist, or user.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>My SoundCloud Player</title>
  <style>
    /* Optional: make the player responsive */
    .sc-player {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 600px;
      height: 166px;   /* default height for a track widget */
      border: none;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>My Favorite Track</h2>

<iframe
  class="sc-player"
  scrolling="no"
  frameborder="no"
  allow="autoplay"
  src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&hide_related=false&show_comments=true&show_user=true&show_reposts=false&show_teaser=true&visual=true">
</iframe>

</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `123456789` with the **track ID** you want to embed.*  
You can get the ID from the URL of a track page (`https://soundcloud.com/artist/track-name`) by using the **SoundCloud API** (see the “Search & Get Track ID” section below) or by looking at the embed code that SoundCloud generates when you click **Share → Embed**.

---

## 2️⃣ Full‑Control Player Using the **Widget API**

The widget API lets you programmatically control playback, listen for events, and swap tracks without reloading the page.

### 2.1 Add the widget script

```html
<script src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js"></script>
```

### 2.2 HTML skeleton

```html
<div id="player-container">
  <!-- The iframe will be created by JavaScript -->
</div>

<button id="play">Play</button>
<button id="pause">Pause</button>
<button id="next">Load Next Track</button>
<span id="status"></span>
```

### 2.3 JavaScript (vanilla)

```html
<script>
  // 1️⃣ List of track IDs you want to cycle through (you can fetch these via the API)
  const trackIds = [
    '123456789', // replace with real IDs
    '987654321',
    '1122334455'
  ];
  let current = 0;

  // 2️⃣ Build the iframe URL
  function buildIframeUrl(trackId) {
    const base = 'https://w.soundcloud.com/player/';
    const params = new URLSearchParams({
      url: `https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/${trackId}`,
      color: '#ff5500',
      auto_play: false,
      hide_related: false,
      show_comments: true,
      show_user: true,
      show_reposts: false,
      visual: true
    });
    return `${base}?${params.toString()}`;
  }

  // 3️⃣ Insert the iframe
  const container = document.getElementById('player-container');
  const iframe = document.createElement('iframe');
  iframe.setAttribute('allow', 'autoplay');
  iframe.setAttribute('scrolling', 'no');
  iframe.setAttribute('frameborder', 'no');
  iframe.setAttribute('width', '100%');
  iframe.setAttribute('height', '300');
  iframe.src = buildIframeUrl(trackIds[current]);
  container.appendChild(iframe);

  // 4️⃣ Create the widget object
  const widget = SC.Widget(iframe);

  // 5️⃣ Hook up UI controls
  document.getElementById('play').onclick = () => widget.play();
  document.getElementById('pause').onclick = () => widget.pause();

  document.getElementById('next').onclick = () => {
    current = (current + 1) % trackIds.length;
    widget.load(buildIframeUrl(trackIds[current]), {
      // optional: start playing automatically
      auto_play: true
    });
  };

  // 6️⃣ Listen for events (optional but handy)
  widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.PLAY, () => {
    document.getElementById('status').textContent = '▶️ Playing';
  });
  widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.PAUSE, () => {
    document.getElementById('status').textContent = '⏸️ Paused';
  });
  widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.FINISH, () => {
    document.getElementById('status').textContent = '✅ Finished';
  });
</script>
```

**What this does**

* Loads a SoundCloud track into an iframe.
* Gives you `play()`, `pause()`, `load()` and many other methods (`seekTo`, `setVolume`, `getCurrentSound`, etc.).
* Fires events you can react to (e.g., update UI when a track ends).

### 2.4 Using a framework (React example)

If you’re using React, you can wrap the widget in a component:

```jsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import './SoundCloudPlayer.css'; // optional styling

const SoundCloudPlayer = ({ trackId }) => {
  const iframeRef = useRef(null);
  const widgetRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const src = `https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=${encodeURIComponent(
      `https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/${trackId}`
    )}&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&visual=true`;

    iframeRef.current.src = src;
    widgetRef.current = window.SC.Widget(iframeRef.current);
  }, [trackId]);

  const play = () => widgetRef.current?.play();
  const pause = () => widgetRef.current?.pause();

  return (
    <div className="sc-wrapper">
      <iframe
        ref={iframeRef}
        width="100%"
        height="300"
        scrolling="no"
        frameBorder="no"
        allow="autoplay"
      />
      <div className="controls">
        <button onClick={play}>Play</button>
        <button onClick={pause}>Pause</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default SoundCloudPlayer;
```

---

## 3️⃣ Full‑Featured Integration (Search, Auth, Private Tracks)

If you need **dynamic content** (search results, user‑specific playlists, private tracks) you’ll have to talk to the SoundCloud **REST API**. The flow is:

1. **Register a SoundCloud app** → get a **client_id** (and client_secret if you need OAuth).  
   *Go to https://soundcloud.com/you/apps → “Register a new application”.*  
2. **(Optional) Authenticate the user** if you want to play their private tracks or let them like/follow.  
3. **Search / fetch track metadata** → obtain the `track_id` and the **stream URL** (or just feed the ID to the widget).  
4. **Play** using the widget (recommended) or, for advanced use‑cases, use the **stream URL** with the HTML5 `<audio>` element (requires a valid OAuth token for private tracks).

Below is a minimal example that **searches for a keyword**, displays the first 5 results, and lets the visitor click a result to load it into the widget.

### 3.1 Get a client ID

```js
const CLIENT_ID = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID_HERE'; // <-- replace with your own
```

### 3.2 Search function (uses the `searchContent` tool you have)

We’ll call the tool to fetch tracks matching a query.

```javascript
async function searchTracks(query, limit = 5) {
  const response = await fetch(`https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks?client_id=${CLIENT_ID}&q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&limit=${limit}`);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Search failed');
  return response.json(); // array of track objects
}
```

> **Note:** If you prefer to use the provided `searchContent` function (the sandboxed tool), you can call it like this:

```javascript
await searchContent({ query: 'lofi chill', limit: 5, type: 'track' });
```

The tool returns the same JSON structure you’d get from the public API.

### 3.3 Full HTML/JS example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>SoundCloud Search & Play</title>
  <script src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: sans-serif; max-width: 800px; margin: auto; }
    #results { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
    #results li { margin: 8px 0; cursor: pointer; }
    #results li:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Search SoundCloud</h1>
<input id="searchBox" type="text" placeholder="Enter a keyword…" />
<button id="searchBtn">Search</button>

<ul id="results"></ul>

<h2>Now Playing</h2>
<div id="playerContainer"></div>

<script>
  const CLIENT_ID = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID_HERE'; // <-- put your client id here

  const searchBox = document.getElementById('searchBox');
  const searchBtn = document.getElementById('searchBtn');
  const resultsUl = document.getElementById('results');
  const playerContainer = document.getElementById('playerContainer');

  let widget = null; // will hold the SC.Widget instance

  async function doSearch() {
    const query = searchBox.value.trim();
    if (!query) return;

    // 1️⃣ Call the public API (or the sandboxed tool)
    const tracks = await fetch(`https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks?client_id=${CLIENT_ID}&q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&limit=10`)
      .then(r => r.json());

    // 2️⃣ Render results
    resultsUl.innerHTML = '';
    tracks.forEach(track => {
      const li = document.createElement('li');
      li.textContent = `${track.title} – ${track.user.username}`;
      li.dataset.id = track.id;
      li.onclick = () => loadTrack(track.id);
      resultsUl.appendChild(li);
    });
  }

  function loadTrack(trackId) {
    // Build the widget URL
    const src = `https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=${encodeURIComponent('https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/' + trackId)}&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=true&visual=true`;

    // If we already have an iframe, just change its src; otherwise create it.
    let iframe = playerContainer.querySelector('iframe');
    if (!iframe) {
      iframe = document.createElement('iframe');
      iframe.setAttribute('allow', 'autoplay');
      iframe.setAttribute('scrolling', 'no');
      iframe.setAttribute('frameborder', 'no');
      iframe.width = '100%';
      iframe.height = '300';
      playerContainer.appendChild(iframe);
    }
    iframe.src = src;

    // (Re)create the widget object
    widget = SC.Widget(iframe);
  }

  // Hook up UI
  searchBtn.onclick = doSearch;
  searchBox.addEventListener('keypress', e => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter') doSearch();
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

**What you get**

* A search box that queries SoundCloud for the entered keyword.
* A list of the first 10 matching tracks.
* Clicking a result loads that track into a **widget player** that starts playing automatically.

### 3.4 Authenticating a user (OAuth)

If you need to let users log in (e.g., to play their private tracks, like songs, or create playlists), follow these steps:

1. **Redirect the user to the SoundCloud auth endpoint**

```html
<a href="https://soundcloud.com/connect?client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID&redirect_uri=YOUR_CALLBACK_URL&response_type=code&scope=non-expiring">
  Log in with SoundCloud
</a>
```

2. **Handle the callback** – SoundCloud will redirect to `YOUR_CALLBACK_URL?code=AUTH_CODE`. Exchange that code for an access token:

```bash
POST https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token
  client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID
  client_secret=YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET
  grant_type=authorization_code
  redirect_uri=YOUR_CALLBACK_URL
  code=AUTH_CODE
```

3. **Store the `access_token`** (usually in a secure HTTP‑only cookie or server‑side session).  
   Use it for any API call that requires user authentication:

```js
fetch(`https://api.soundcloud.com/me?oauth_token=${access_token}`)
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(user => console.log('Logged in as', user.username));
```

4. **Play private tracks** – the widget can accept an `oauth_token` parameter:

```html
<iframe src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/PRIVATE_TRACK_ID&oauth_token=USER_ACCESS_TOKEN&auto_play=true"></iframe>
```

> **Tip:** For most public playback scenarios you **don’t need OAuth**; a client‑id is enough. Only request the `non‑expiring` scope if you truly need a long‑lived token.

